<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narbonne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Narbo Martius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narbonne is a city in the Aude <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast. Founded as Narbo Martius in 118 BCE, the first Roman colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, it became the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a> and stood on the Via Domitia. It was later a Visigothic and, briefly, an Umayyad stronghold before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> took it in 759.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narbonne.jpg" alt="Narbonne" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benh LIEU SONG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narbonne_Cathedrale_Saint_Just_et_Saint_Pasteur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Narbonne lies about 15 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and was historically a prosperous port. In Roman times the river Aude, then called the Atax, divided into two branches, and the southern one followed the course of today's Canal de la Robine past the colony. The colony was founded near an older settlement on the hill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montlaures">Montlaurès</a>, about 4 kilometres to the north, where an oppidum dates back to the 6th century BCE. Hecataeus of Miletus, in the 5th century BCE, called the place a Celtic harbour and marketplace. The Via Domitia, the first Roman road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> here and met the Via Aquitania, which ran towards the Atlantic through Tolosa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Burdigala</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Narbonne rose as a competitor to Massilia. Julius Caesar settled veterans of his 10th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> there and tried to develop its port while Massilia supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. The province it headed, Gallia Transalpina, was later renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a> after the city. It had an estimated 30,000 to 50,000 inhabitants.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a>, a local military leader handed the city to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in 462 in exchange for support, ending Roman rule there. It became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a>, which held out against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vouille">Battle of Vouillé</a> in 507. Muslim forces took the region from 719 and called it Arbūnah, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> conquered Narbonne in 759.</p><p>French kings took effective control of the region only in the early 13th century, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a>. Narbonne became a major centre of Jewish learning, with an important exegetical school in the 11th and 12th centuries and a Jewish community of about 2,000 people in the 12th. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermengarde-of-narbonne">Ermengarde of Narbonne</a>, who ruled from 1134 to 1192, presided over a court where the spirit of courtly love developed.</p><p>The city declined from the 14th century. A change in the course of the Aude silted up its access to the sea, and a flood in 1320 swept away the dam the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had built. The plague and a raid by Edward, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>, did further harm. Its cathedral was never finished, partly because completing it would have meant demolishing the city wall. From the sixteenth century the townspeople worked to keep the old river channel navigable, and the resulting Canal de la Robine was linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canal-du-midi">Canal du Midi</a> in 1776.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Narbonne is a commune and subprefecture in the Aude department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occitania">Occitania</a>, the largest commune of the department though not its capital, which is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>. Its sights include the Cathedral of Saints Justus and Pastor, the Palace of the Archbishops, the Roman Horreum, a cryptoporticus once used as a grain warehouse, and remains of the Via Domitia in the city centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narbonne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As royal authority in West Francia weakened, the counts gathered rights over Quercy, Albi, Nîmes and <b>Narbonne</b>, and in the tenth century Raymond III Pons appears in some documents with the title of duke of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The decisive Battle of Sanluri in 1409, won by Martin the Younger, King of Sicily, broke Arborean power, and the remaining rights were bought from William III of <b>Narbonne</b> in 1420.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Alaric they sacked Rome in 410, carrying off Galla Placidia, the sister of the emperor Honorius; his successor Athaulf (410–415) took <b>Narbonne</b> and Toulouse in 413 and married Placidia, after which Honorius enlisted the Visigoths against the Vandals, Alans and Suebi in Hispania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 118 BCE Rome founded the colony of <b>Narbo Martius</b> next to Montlaurès, which became the capital of Gallia Narbonensis.</blockquote><a href="/state/elisyces">Elisyces</a></li><li><blockquote>Viscounts appear at <b>Narbonne</b> around 900 as deputies of the counts of Toulouse, who had inherited authority over the former Carolingian march of Septimania.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-narbonne">Viscounty of Narbonne</a></li></ul></section>
